digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-57012-4
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Investigate photon irradiation of an illuminated Cu2Se-based surface as a candidate platform for direct atomic-scale imaging of photoinduced surface reconstruction and possible semiconductor-to-metal transition behavior, with emphasis on whether the system can provide novel cross-material PSMT evidence and mechanistic insight rather than presupposing a specific Cu2Se/Cu(111), metallic-Cu-monolayer, interlayer Cu/Se-exchange, or excited-state transition-barrier pathway.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When an impactful photoinduced transition lacks direct atomic-scale evidence, a scientifically meaningful proposal is to select a plausible illuminated material platform, image any structural pathway directly, and connect the observed reconstruction to electronic phase conversion and mechanistic constraints."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Because controllable reconstruction triggers are important and photoinduced reconstruction is scarcely reported despite being controllable and remotely operable, photoinduced surface reconstruction is a valuable trigger class to investigate."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Photoinduced semiconductor-to-metal transitions are impactful research phenomena because they have significant application potential and fundamental relevance to photodynamics, excitations, and electronic-atomic structure coupling."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Cu2Se can be treated as a semiconducting two-dimensional chalcogenide candidate because Cu2Se is intrinsically semiconducting and thin Cu2Se flakes are considered a typical two-dimensional transition-metal chalcogenide."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Reported photoinduced surface reconstructions generally involve structural rearrangements such as lateral-atomic, interlayer-spacing, or surface-dipole changes while keeping the surface-layer chemical composition essentially unchanged."];
  "n1" [label="(1,0,0) Currently, photoinduced semiconductor-to-metal transitions (PSMTs) have significant application potential (e.g., information storage, sensing, optoelectronics, optical switches)."];
  "n10" [label="(11,0,0) Currently, surface reconstruction strongly affects chemical/physical properties and matters for catalysis, sensing, surface engineering, device design, and biomedical technologies."];
  "n11" [label="(12,0,0) Currently, regulating and understanding surface reconstruction is critical for both fundamental and applied research."];
  "n12" [label="(13,0,0) Currently, surface reconstruction can be triggered by multiple approaches (cleaning, cleavage, adsorption, reactions)."];
  "n13" [label="(14,0,0) Currently, photoinduced surface reconstruction is scarcely reported even though it is controllable, remotely operable, and interoperable with other photo-techniques."];
  "n14" [label="(15,0,0) Currently, in all reported photoinduced surface-reconstruction cases, the surface-layer chemical composition remains essentially unchanged."];
  "n15" [label="(16,0,0) Currently, Cu2Se is an intrinsic semiconductor."];
  "n16" [label="(17,0,0) Currently, thin Cu2Se flakes are considered a typical 2D transition-metal chalcogenide."];
  "n17" [label="(18,0,0) Currently, photon–2D-material interactions are of great interest due to unique optical/photoelectrical properties and device potential (sensing, photocatalysis, optoelectronics)."];
  "n2" [label="(2,0,0) Currently, PSMT research is also fundamental because it reveals photodynamics/excitations and links between electronic structure and atomic structure."];
  "n20" [label="(0,0,0) If all known PSMTs are within one material and there is no precedent across different materials, then demonstrating a cross-material PSMT would be novel and fill a gap."];
  "n22" [label="(0,0,0) If photoinduced surface reconstruction is scarcely reported but highly controllable, then exploring photoinduced surface reconstruction mechanisms is a promising approach to regulate surfaces."];
  "n23" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given photoinduced surface reconstruction is scarcely reported and exploring it is promising, therefore a research opportunity is to discover and characterize a photoinduced surface reconstruction pathway."];
  "n24" [label="(0,0,0) If Cu2Se is a semiconducting 2D material and light strongly interacts with 2D materials, then Cu2Se-based surfaces under illumination are plausible platforms to search for photoinduced electronic/structural transitions."];
  "n3" [label="(3,0,0) Currently, in most cases PSMTs are temporary and disappear when irradiation is switched off."];
  "n35" [label="(0,0,0) If PSMT is often temporary but sometimes can be sustained via structural conversion, then identifying transitions with controllable reversibility/stability modes is important for applications and mechanism understanding."];
  "n36" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given PSMTs are often temporary and rarely sustained via conversion, therefore studying controllable reversibility/stability in a PSMT is a valuable research direction."];
  "n37" [label="(0,0,0) If surface reconstruction strongly affects properties and is critical to regulate/understand, then investigating controllable triggers of reconstruction is important."];
  "n39" [label="(0,0,0) If evidence for PSMTs has mainly relied on indirect probes, then developing direct real-space atomic-scale observation is necessary to validate mechanisms."];
  "n4" [label="(4,0,0) Currently, in rare cases a PSMT can be sustained, usually via crystalline↔amorphous conversion within a material."];
  "n40" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given PSMT evidence is mainly indirect and direct real-space proof is absent, therefore the field needs direct real-space atomic-scale validation of PSMT mechanisms."];
  "n5" [label="(6,0,0) Currently, evidence for PSMTs has mainly relied on indirect probes (conductivity changes, diffraction spectra, or theoretical simulations)."];
  "n6" [label="(7,0,0) Currently, direct real-space proof of PSMT at atomic/molecular scale is absent."];
  "n7" [label="(8,0,0) Currently, all reported PSMTs occur between two structural phases of the same material."];
  "n8" [label="(9,0,0) Currently, there is no precedent for PSMTs between two different materials."];
  "n9" [label="(10,0,0) Currently, if a surface undergoes reconstruction, then its lateral atomic arrangement changes and it is likely accompanied by changes in interlayer spacing and surface dipole."];
